    "proof_tex": "The legal spectral tuples form a closed subset of the compact product of the amplitude interval, exponent interval, and nuisance compactum: for each fixed \\(\\omega\\), the determinant inequality is closed, and intersection over \\(\\omega\\in\\mathbb R^d\\) preserves closedness.  The map from such a tuple to\n\\[\n \\left(\\frac{AK_d(p)\\sqrt N h_N^{2p-1}}{\\sqrt{c_Xc_W}},p\\right)\n\\]\nis continuous because \\(h_N,c_X,c_W,K_d(p)>0\\).  Its image \\(\\Lambda_N^{\\mathrm{phys}}\\) is therefore compact.\n\nThe atomlessness and strict increase are the finite-\\(N\\) cases proved in the compactification lemma.  If \\((\\lambda_n,p_n)\\to(\\lambda,p)\\), coupling with the same \\(G_N\\) and using continuity of \\(q_N\\) gives almost-sure convergence of the displayed statistic.  Atomlessness gives convergence of its distribution functions at every threshold.  Strict increase then gives convergence of the unique \\((1-\\alpha)\\)-quantiles: if a subsequence of quantiles stayed below or above the limiting quantile, evaluation at an intermediate continuity point would contradict the definition of a quantile.  Thus the quantile map is continuous, and compactness makes its maximum attainable.\n\nLet \\((\\lambda_*,p_*)\\) be a maximizer.  Every parameter pair has probability at least \\(1-\\alpha\\) at the maximum critical value, while continuity and strict increase give probability exactly \\(1-\\alpha\\) for the maximizing pair.  This proves (20).\n\nFor (21), the cone contains the complete line \\(\\{bq_N(p):b\\in\\mathbb R\\}\\).  Therefore\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n d_{H_N}(G_N+\\lambda q_N(p),\\mathcal C_N)^2\n &\\le \\inf_{b\\in\\mathbb R}\n   \\|G_N+\\lambda q_N(p)-bq_N(p)\\|_{H_N}^2\\\\\n &=\\inf_{a\\in\\mathbb R}\\|G_N-aq_N(p)\\|_{H_N}^2.\n \\end{aligned}                                                               \\tag{22}\n\\]\nAfter whitening, the last expression is the squared Euclidean norm of the projection of a standard three-dimensional Gaussian onto a two-dimensional subspace, hence has the \\(\\chi^2_2\\) distribution.  This proves the upper bound.  Positivity and its uniform version follow from atomlessness at zero, strict increase, and the compactification argument: a sequence of zero or vanishing \\((1-\\alpha)\\)-quantiles would have a compactified limit with positive probability at distance zero, contrary to (18).",
